<script>
    struct $state {
        current_snippet: $bindable(test1),
    }

    impl $state {
        fn switch_snippet(&mut self) {
            if self.current_snippet == test1 {
                self.current_snippet = test2;
            } else {
                self.current_snippet = test1;
            }
        }
    }
</script>

<button onclick={switch_snippet}>
    Switch Snippet
</button>
<#snippet test1(n: String)>
    <p>{n}</p>
</snippet>
<#snippet test2(n: String)>
    <h1>{n}</h1>
</snippet>

{#each (0..5) as i}
    <#snippet test_each(n: String)> <!-- this is a separate snippet for each i -->
        <p>{n} from snippet {i}</p>
    </snippet>
    <button onclick={|| current_snippet = test_each;}>Snippet {i}</button>
{/each}

{@render current_snippet("Hello, world!") }

<!--
Notes about snippets:
 - Snippets are rendered with the scope which they're defined in
 - When a snippet is passed as a prop or a bindable, it uses
    the scope and state of the defining component, but the props of the calling component.
 - Snippets, as long as they have the same props, have the same type and can be set to
    state variables.
 - Only snippets in the top level (`()`) scope are available on init to be assigned to state variables
 - Snippets defined in some scope can only be rendered or passed down in that or a child scope,
    but not in a parent or sibling scope.
 - A snippet can be passed as a prop to a state function from within a child scope, and acts like a
    closure that captures the state of the defining scope at the time it's called, but uses the props
    of the calling scope.
 - Snippets cannot be solely owned by the rendering component because they need to maintain reactivity
    with the defining component, however there may be several instances with various props and different
    reactivity update masks, so separate instances do need to be maintained

Ideas:
 - having references is problematic because the lifetime of child components is not guaranteed in the bindable case,
    implies one of the following:
        - uses of state & scope must be cloned, requiring Clone bounds on all state and scope types
        - the snippet content must be stored in some kind of Rc<RefCell<>>
        - uses of state & scope must be re-rendered into contents (not possible if expressions contain both state and props)
        - references must be re-acquired on each re-render, meaning traversal into defining component
            - could add a special dirty flag related to snippet source re-renders to acquire references
            - still could cause lifetime issues if the references are stored anywhere
 - a new kind of trait which takes in snippet props and, based on 

|props| { |state, scope| snippet } -> |props| { snippet_closure } -> evaluated_snippet
        - however it's not a true closure because it has deep reactivity in both the defining and calling scopes
-->